1
00:00:56,222 --> 00:01:03,188
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

2
00:01:04,898 --> 00:01:06,983
{\an8}EPISODE 1

3
00:01:49,567 --> 00:01:52,278
{\an8}SPRING 1932, GYEONGSEONG

4
00:01:52,779 --> 00:01:54,155
Rickshaw coming through!

5
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:13,133
Damn it.

6
00:02:17,470 --> 00:02:19,889
Something good
better be coming my way today.

7
00:02:22,308 --> 00:02:23,726
- Out of the way.
- Move.

8
00:02:23,810 --> 00:02:25,270
- Out of the way.
- Extra!

9
00:02:47,917 --> 00:02:51,379
This stop is Keijo, Keijo Station.

10
00:02:52,088 --> 00:02:55,383
Please ensure you have
all your belongings before you get off.

11
00:02:56,217 --> 00:02:59,762
Thank you for using our service today.

12
00:03:24,412 --> 00:03:25,663
I'll get your bag down.

13
00:03:26,831 --> 00:03:28,166
It's okay.

14
00:03:28,249 --> 00:03:29,542
I'll do it.

15
00:03:51,397 --> 00:03:52,315
So-ran!

16
00:04:32,981 --> 00:04:35,233
Does he not watch where he's going?

17
00:04:35,316 --> 00:04:36,734
Thank you.

18
00:04:40,947 --> 00:04:42,824
- Excuse me.
- Yes?

19
00:04:42,907 --> 00:04:45,994
This is Changgyeongwon, right?

20
00:04:46,077 --> 00:04:47,912
Well, Changgyeongwon is…

21
00:04:47,996 --> 00:04:51,165
Go straight to the left, then turn right.

22
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:53,209
- Thank you.
- My pleasure.

23
00:05:06,264 --> 00:05:07,473
Thank you.

24
00:05:08,599 --> 00:05:10,560
You have something here.

25
00:05:13,479 --> 00:05:14,731
Thank you.

26
00:05:32,999 --> 00:05:35,335
Thank you. It slipped out of my hand.

27
00:06:13,539 --> 00:06:15,500
Who's here? What's all this about?

28
00:06:16,626 --> 00:06:19,754
It's the inspector general
of the Government-General of Joseon.

29
00:06:20,838 --> 00:06:22,173
The inspector general…

30
00:06:23,007 --> 00:06:24,217
Is he someone important?

31
00:06:24,926 --> 00:06:26,094
Look, miss.

32
00:06:26,177 --> 00:06:31,641
The inspector general
is the Government-General's number two.

33
00:06:32,767 --> 00:06:35,103
You don't even know
what an inspector general is.

34
00:06:35,186 --> 00:06:38,231
How could you know
what "number two" means?

35
00:06:38,314 --> 00:06:39,524
Of course I do.

36
00:06:39,607 --> 00:06:42,360
It means he's second only
to the Governor-General.

37
00:06:42,443 --> 00:06:44,445
Well, well. How do you understand English?

38
00:06:44,529 --> 00:06:45,947
And you're a woman at that.

39
00:06:47,281 --> 00:06:49,033
I speak a little English myself.

40
00:06:49,117 --> 00:06:50,076
Yeah, sure.

41
00:07:11,055 --> 00:07:12,098
Thank you.

42
00:07:15,810 --> 00:07:18,688
Welcome to Joseon, Inspector General.

43
00:07:18,771 --> 00:07:20,857
Thank you, Interpreter Suzuki.

44
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:22,650
- Have you been well?
- Yes, sir.

45
00:07:22,733 --> 00:07:26,988
You look about ten years younger
than you did in Tokyo last month.

46
00:07:27,071 --> 00:07:29,866
They say Keijo is quite a place.
Seems the rumors are true.

47
00:07:30,408 --> 00:07:33,202
It's improved quite a bit
since you were last here.

48
00:07:34,829 --> 00:07:35,872
That's good to hear.

49
00:07:38,374 --> 00:07:39,208
Fukuda!

50
00:07:40,793 --> 00:07:41,711
No,

51
00:07:41,794 --> 00:07:44,922
I should call you Chief of Police
of the Government-General of Joseon now.

52
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:47,049
Has it been ten years?

53
00:07:47,925 --> 00:07:48,968
Yes, sir.

54
00:07:49,051 --> 00:07:51,220
It's an honor to serve you again.

55
00:07:53,931 --> 00:07:54,807
This is…

56
00:08:02,648 --> 00:08:03,524
Who is that?

57
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:09,113
He's a WWP News Keijo correspondent,
the one I mentioned in Tokyo.

58
00:08:10,239 --> 00:08:11,574
I see.

59
00:08:11,657 --> 00:08:13,493
You said he's Joseon-American?

60
00:08:13,576 --> 00:08:18,498
Yes, he's from Hawaii. He was based
in Shanghai, China until three months ago.

61
00:08:18,581 --> 00:08:20,917
He's quite friendly
toward our Government-General.

62
00:08:22,335 --> 00:08:25,004
Then have him come over.
I should introduce myself.

63
00:08:25,087 --> 00:08:28,174
He's an American reporter.
With what's happening in Manchukuo,

64
00:08:28,257 --> 00:08:31,135
he's someone our home country
would keep a close eye on.

65
00:08:32,803 --> 00:08:34,055
We should keep him on our side.

66
00:08:34,639 --> 00:08:35,556
Yes, sir.

67
00:08:36,390 --> 00:08:37,642
Mr. Yoo Philip!

68
00:08:40,478 --> 00:08:41,562
One moment.

69
00:08:54,659 --> 00:08:56,410
A Chinese couple?

70
00:08:58,704 --> 00:08:59,664
Nice to meet you.

71
00:08:59,747 --> 00:09:03,584
I'm Yoo Philip, Keijo correspondent
for the American WWP News Agency.

72
00:09:03,668 --> 00:09:05,795
It's a great honor to meet you.

73
00:09:05,878 --> 00:09:07,171
No, the honor is mine.

74
00:09:07,255 --> 00:09:11,259
Nothing's scarier
than a journalist's pen these days.

75
00:09:11,342 --> 00:09:13,719
I hope you'll be gentle with us.

76
00:09:17,139 --> 00:09:18,057
Please don't worry.

77
00:09:21,769 --> 00:09:24,981
Allow me to introduce my wife.
She's Chinese.

78
00:09:26,274 --> 00:09:29,569
I first came to Keijo three months ago,

79
00:09:29,652 --> 00:09:32,154
and she arrived from Shanghai just now.

80
00:09:33,447 --> 00:09:35,199
My congratulations on your reunion.

81
00:09:36,576 --> 00:09:38,327
And congratulations on the little one.

82
00:09:39,412 --> 00:09:42,039
Thank you. My wife cannot speak Japanese.

83
00:09:44,292 --> 00:09:46,794
He kindly congratulated us
on our baby on the way.

84
00:09:49,046 --> 00:09:50,089
Thank you.

85
00:09:50,673 --> 00:09:51,882
You speak the Joseon language.

86
00:09:52,925 --> 00:09:55,928
{\an8}Now that you're in Gyeongseong,
you should learn Japanese.

87
00:09:56,637 --> 00:09:58,973
Sir, I must say
you speak the language so well.

88
00:10:00,308 --> 00:10:01,851
Does it sound okay?

89
00:10:01,934 --> 00:10:04,770
I left Joseon more than ten years ago,
so I've forgotten quite a lot.

90
00:10:04,854 --> 00:10:06,480
You still speak it better than I do.

91
00:10:09,483 --> 00:10:11,819
Let's get together for a meal
sometime soon.

92
00:10:11,902 --> 00:10:14,071
Yes, I was actually hoping
to have you over.

93
00:10:14,155 --> 00:10:16,115
How about at our place?

94
00:10:16,866 --> 00:10:18,075
Would that be all right?

95
00:10:18,159 --> 00:10:19,201
We would be honored.

96
00:10:19,285 --> 00:10:22,830
How about next Thursday?

97
00:10:22,913 --> 00:10:23,956
Very well.

98
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:26,208
All right, we'll talk more then.

99
00:10:27,209 --> 00:10:28,336
Is Hideo not here yet?

100
00:10:29,670 --> 00:10:31,213
My son and daughter came with me.

101
00:10:34,634 --> 00:10:36,510
Is that a chair with wheels?

102
00:10:37,928 --> 00:10:39,347
Please, no pushing.

103
00:10:41,849 --> 00:10:43,017
Excuse me.

104
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:44,769
I didn't know you had a daughter.

105
00:10:44,852 --> 00:10:46,604
I have a 12-year-old girl.

106
00:10:46,687 --> 00:10:49,231
She's excited.
It's her first time in Gyeongseong.

107
00:10:49,315 --> 00:10:50,483
That's natural at her age.

108
00:10:53,402 --> 00:10:54,862
- What's the matter?
- My bag…

109
00:11:00,868 --> 00:11:02,286
What's wrong?

110
00:11:19,637 --> 00:11:21,389
We'll be conducting a brief inspection.

111
00:11:21,472 --> 00:11:22,932
Form a single line, everyone!

112
00:11:23,015 --> 00:11:24,850
What's going on? Is it a pickpocket?

113
00:11:31,607 --> 00:11:32,733
I think it's that way.

114
00:11:39,532 --> 00:11:40,574
A bag was stolen?

115
00:11:42,201 --> 00:11:44,704
Did someone steal a bag in this crowd?

116
00:11:57,508 --> 00:12:01,095
I left something in my seat,
but this isn't where I was sitting.

117
00:12:12,148 --> 00:12:13,023
Oh my.

118
00:12:13,107 --> 00:12:14,692
Babe, what was your name?

119
00:12:14,775 --> 00:12:15,609
What?

120
00:12:15,693 --> 00:12:17,611
Never mind. "Gorgeous" will do.

121
00:12:18,362 --> 00:12:19,989
- Oh dear. Excuse us.
- Excuse me.

122
00:12:27,788 --> 00:12:29,331
{\an8}FIRST-CLASS COMPARTMENT

123
00:12:40,843 --> 00:12:42,386
- I'm sorry.
- I'm sorry.

124
00:12:43,554 --> 00:12:45,014
- I'm sorry.
- I'm sorry.

125
00:12:48,309 --> 00:12:49,602
Are you from Joseon?

126
00:12:55,065 --> 00:12:57,735
Sorry, I just assumed
because you spoke the language.

127
00:12:57,818 --> 00:12:59,403
{\an8}I'm also from mainland Japan.

128
00:13:03,657 --> 00:13:04,533
One second.

129
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:10,873
Thank you.

130
00:13:19,799 --> 00:13:20,883
Come on, let's go.

131
00:13:22,843 --> 00:13:23,886
Please go ahead.

132
00:13:23,969 --> 00:13:26,430
Go this way. That way is closed off.

133
00:13:28,098 --> 00:13:28,974
Well then.

134
00:13:29,642 --> 00:13:33,187
No, you can't go through that door.

135
00:13:34,897 --> 00:13:37,149
I said you can't go that way.

136
00:13:37,233 --> 00:13:38,108
Wait…

137
00:13:59,547 --> 00:14:00,589
Excuse me.

138
00:14:02,007 --> 00:14:04,176
Yes? Me?

139
00:14:05,010 --> 00:14:07,388
Haven't you seen a picture of the princess
in the newspaper?

140
00:14:08,556 --> 00:14:09,431
Sorry?

141
00:14:10,432 --> 00:14:13,018
Look at all the crowd
waiting to welcome her.

142
00:14:14,436 --> 00:14:17,815
Doesn't it occur to you
that she might actually be the princess?

143
00:14:24,154 --> 00:14:25,406
I'm sorry.

144
00:14:25,489 --> 00:14:26,323
I'm sorry.

145
00:14:26,407 --> 00:14:28,033
Though of course she isn't.

146
00:14:30,452 --> 00:14:31,453
Well then.

147
00:14:32,705 --> 00:14:34,206
Hey, hold on.

148
00:14:42,423 --> 00:14:44,341
I really thought she was the princess.

149
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:53,642
FIRST CLASS

150
00:15:19,460 --> 00:15:20,377
Thank you.

151
00:15:29,261 --> 00:15:30,638
A bag was stolen?

152
00:15:31,263 --> 00:15:33,974
Yes, so you can exchange greetings later.

153
00:15:34,058 --> 00:15:35,059
It's cold.

154
00:15:36,685 --> 00:15:37,561
Are you cold?

155
00:15:37,645 --> 00:15:38,854
Hold on, Hanako.

156
00:15:52,534 --> 00:15:55,287
No, you can't go through that door.

157
00:15:57,206 --> 00:15:59,583
Where are you going?

158
00:15:59,667 --> 00:16:01,543
Hold on, Hanako.

159
00:16:01,627 --> 00:16:03,420
I said I'm cold.

160
00:16:09,718 --> 00:16:10,594
I'm sorry.

161
00:16:10,678 --> 00:16:13,847
Is it always winter in Joseon?

162
00:16:13,931 --> 00:16:16,642
It's March, but it's still too cold.

163
00:16:17,977 --> 00:16:18,811
No.

164
00:16:21,897 --> 00:16:24,692
Spring will soon be here in Joseon too.

165
00:16:28,570 --> 00:16:31,824
Could I speak with the police?

166
00:16:33,659 --> 00:16:35,911
Why don't you take a taxi?

167
00:16:35,995 --> 00:16:37,621
How about a tour of Gyeongseong?

168
00:16:37,705 --> 00:16:41,583
Joseon Shrine, Gyeongseong Shrine,
Jangchungdan Shrine, then turn, okay?

169
00:16:41,667 --> 00:16:42,710
Do you go to America too?

170
00:16:42,793 --> 00:16:44,837
What? America?

171
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:46,505
Welcome!

172
00:16:47,214 --> 00:16:48,674
I'm going to America.

173
00:16:48,757 --> 00:16:51,760
Sure, ma'am, I'll take you there.

174
00:16:55,347 --> 00:16:56,557
Here we go.

175
00:17:00,644 --> 00:17:04,314
You were taking so long. I thought
you went to America without telling me.

176
00:17:04,398 --> 00:17:06,358
In spirit, I'm already there.

177
00:17:23,125 --> 00:17:28,297
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

178
00:17:50,069 --> 00:17:51,111
APPRAISAL

179
00:17:53,030 --> 00:17:53,906
Damn it.

180
00:17:58,118 --> 00:17:59,036
Hey.

181
00:18:01,663 --> 00:18:02,623
Goodbye.

182
00:18:03,582 --> 00:18:06,210
JONGNO PAWNSHOP

183
00:18:08,128 --> 00:18:09,254
Come on.

184
00:18:17,638 --> 00:18:19,765
Hey, did you see
who arrived at the station?

185
00:18:19,848 --> 00:18:21,809
He's the Government-General's number two.

186
00:18:21,892 --> 00:18:23,227
Number two<i>?</i>

187
00:18:23,310 --> 00:18:24,520
What, English again?

188
00:18:27,648 --> 00:18:30,901
If I'd grown up in a missionary's house
like you did,

189
00:18:30,984 --> 00:18:32,319
I'd have learned English too.

190
00:18:32,945 --> 00:18:35,447
{\an8}Do you think
being a live-in maid was easy?

191
00:18:36,198 --> 00:18:38,075
{\an8}Of course I know it was hard.

192
00:18:38,158 --> 00:18:41,495
When I first met you,
you were crying outside that house.

193
00:18:41,578 --> 00:18:44,790
You were accused of stealing food
and got beaten for it.

194
00:18:45,415 --> 00:18:46,542
And the next day,

195
00:18:46,625 --> 00:18:49,461
the missionary felt bad
and gave you rice, didn't he?

196
00:18:49,545 --> 00:18:52,714
Right. But they weren't mistaken.

197
00:18:52,798 --> 00:18:54,091
Wait, what?

198
00:18:54,591 --> 00:18:57,761
I really did steal their bread.

199
00:18:58,595 --> 00:19:00,472
What? You actually did?

200
00:19:00,556 --> 00:19:02,933
Yes, I was starving.

201
00:19:03,016 --> 00:19:05,227
I shed a few tears,
and they all fell for it.

202
00:19:05,310 --> 00:19:08,313
Hey, how could you not have
told me that all this time?

203
00:19:08,397 --> 00:19:10,524
You never asked, dummy.

204
00:19:10,607 --> 00:19:12,401
Would you have told me if I'd asked?

205
00:19:12,943 --> 00:19:13,861
Answer me.

206
00:19:19,658 --> 00:19:21,785
Hey, Lee Ga-kyung!

207
00:19:33,839 --> 00:19:35,048
You were taking forever.

208
00:19:36,383 --> 00:19:38,177
I thought you'd run off again.

209
00:19:45,100 --> 00:19:46,894
Take it all out.

210
00:19:46,977 --> 00:19:49,479
If I catch you swiping anything again,
you're dead.

211
00:19:50,147 --> 00:19:52,357
Cigarettes. Look at you.

212
00:19:52,441 --> 00:19:53,275
Go eat.

213
00:19:56,528 --> 00:19:58,614
Why'd you bring pine cones?

214
00:19:58,697 --> 00:20:00,991
You idiot. All you brought is junk.

215
00:20:01,617 --> 00:20:03,619
What do I do with you?

216
00:20:06,288 --> 00:20:07,289
Damn it.

217
00:20:07,372 --> 00:20:08,457
Go on, eat.

218
00:20:09,875 --> 00:20:10,959
One jeon?

219
00:20:11,752 --> 00:20:12,628
Work hard!

220
00:20:13,212 --> 00:20:14,546
- Yes, sir.
- Now eat.

221
00:20:17,257 --> 00:20:18,258
Is this it?

222
00:20:18,842 --> 00:20:20,886
If anything turns up in your pockets,

223
00:20:20,969 --> 00:20:22,179
I'll beat you for every jeon.

224
00:20:25,390 --> 00:20:28,810
You little bastard,
how dare you try to sneak some off?

225
00:20:28,894 --> 00:20:29,770
Eat up, asshole.

226
00:20:32,022 --> 00:20:33,357
Why are you empty-handed?

227
00:20:33,941 --> 00:20:36,944
You went to Itsukoshi Department Store.
How come you've got nothing?

228
00:20:37,569 --> 00:20:40,614
Today, there were three policemen…

229
00:20:40,697 --> 00:20:43,033
What? Did you just say "policemen"?

230
00:20:44,368 --> 00:20:45,244
Hey!

231
00:20:46,495 --> 00:20:47,829
What are the police?

232
00:20:47,913 --> 00:20:50,457
The police are Boss's <i>tomodachi.</i>

233
00:20:51,250 --> 00:20:52,251
Did you hear that?

234
00:20:52,334 --> 00:20:54,836
The police are my friends<i>.</i>

235
00:20:55,462 --> 00:20:57,839
So that's no excuse.

236
00:20:57,923 --> 00:20:59,174
You little bastard.

237
00:21:00,050 --> 00:21:01,927
You get nothing to eat today.

238
00:21:06,723 --> 00:21:09,434
What are you, some kind of animal?

239
00:21:09,518 --> 00:21:11,520
That's disgusting.

240
00:21:13,981 --> 00:21:15,232
You got nothing either?

241
00:21:16,358 --> 00:21:17,651
Of course not.

242
00:21:25,617 --> 00:21:26,785
What is this crap?

243
00:21:36,128 --> 00:21:37,296
Here you go.

244
00:21:38,463 --> 00:21:39,548
Thank you.

245
00:21:40,632 --> 00:21:44,261
All of this together
isn't even worth one won.

246
00:21:45,012 --> 00:21:47,848
After I bribe the police
at Gyeongseong Station,

247
00:21:47,931 --> 00:21:49,308
what's left for me?

248
00:21:50,809 --> 00:21:54,271
You stole my money three years ago
and ran off to sneak onto a ship.

249
00:21:54,354 --> 00:21:57,983
And when you were caught at Incheon Port,
what did you promise me?

250
00:21:59,109 --> 00:22:01,153
If you fail to repay one won a day,

251
00:22:02,738 --> 00:22:04,740
or if I catch you sneaking something away…

252
00:22:08,577 --> 00:22:09,828
how were you going to repay me?

253
00:22:11,413 --> 00:22:12,956
Come on, Boss.

254
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:15,459
You know Ga-kyung.
That's not all she's got.

255
00:22:15,542 --> 00:22:16,835
Hold on.

256
00:22:17,502 --> 00:22:18,462
Here's the big one.

257
00:22:20,756 --> 00:22:22,132
You little bastard.

258
00:22:22,215 --> 00:22:23,884
You tried to sneak this away, huh?

259
00:22:23,967 --> 00:22:26,178
No, I was about to give it to you,

260
00:22:26,261 --> 00:22:28,805
but you were being so impatient.

261
00:22:28,889 --> 00:22:31,224
First, let go of this.

262
00:22:31,308 --> 00:22:34,853
If she hurts her wrist and can't work,
who will lose out?

263
00:22:34,936 --> 00:22:35,979
Isn't that right?

264
00:22:36,063 --> 00:22:37,689
Stay out of this!

265
00:22:45,030 --> 00:22:46,239
Don't forget…

266
00:22:49,576 --> 00:22:50,744
what you promised me.

267
00:22:53,955 --> 00:22:54,790
Okay?

268
00:22:55,374 --> 00:22:56,249
Okay.

269
00:22:58,502 --> 00:23:02,172
<i>- Neener-neener, neener-neener</i>
<i>- Neener-neener, neener-neener</i>

270
00:23:02,255 --> 00:23:06,343
<i>- Neener-neener, neener-neener</i>
<i>- Neener-neener, neener-neener</i>

271
00:23:06,426 --> 00:23:10,263
<i>- Kkot-nim can't walk</i>
<i>- Kkot-nim can't walk</i>

272
00:23:10,347 --> 00:23:11,973
<i>- She can't walk</i>
<i>- She can't walk</i>

273
00:23:12,057 --> 00:23:14,393
I'll tell my sister and brother
on you all!

274
00:23:14,476 --> 00:23:16,436
<i>- Go on, tell 'em, Jap!</i>
<i>- Go on, tell 'em, Jap!</i>

275
00:23:16,520 --> 00:23:18,647
<i>- Go to Japan and rot</i>
<i>- Go to Japan and rot</i>

276
00:23:18,730 --> 00:23:20,565
Damn it, I'm not Japanese!

277
00:23:20,649 --> 00:23:22,150
Hey, you brats!

278
00:23:22,943 --> 00:23:23,985
Get over here.

279
00:23:24,945 --> 00:23:26,154
You're at it again.

280
00:23:26,738 --> 00:23:28,115
Get over here! You little punks.

281
00:23:28,740 --> 00:23:31,660
You try picking on Kkot-nim again,
I'll teach you a lesson!

282
00:23:31,743 --> 00:23:33,578
Kkot-nim, did they pick on you again?

283
00:23:33,662 --> 00:23:35,705
I know where you live!

284
00:23:37,457 --> 00:23:41,128
Kkot-nim, it's a big secret,
so I've kept it from you all this time.

285
00:23:41,211 --> 00:23:42,504
But I think it's time you knew.

286
00:23:44,506 --> 00:23:45,382
Listen carefully.

287
00:23:46,174 --> 00:23:48,385
Kkot-nim, your mom and dad

288
00:23:48,468 --> 00:23:50,429
joined Ahn Jung-geun,
the patriotic martyr,

289
00:23:50,512 --> 00:23:53,306
in assassinating Ito Hirobumi and died.

290
00:23:54,099 --> 00:23:57,477
They carried you on their backs, but then
you were injured and could no longer walk.

291
00:23:57,561 --> 00:23:59,104
Wow, for real?

292
00:23:59,187 --> 00:24:01,982
Yeah. So next time they make fun of you--

293
00:24:03,650 --> 00:24:04,860
<i>S…</i>

294
00:24:05,444 --> 00:24:06,653
- S… what?
- S… what?

295
00:24:07,821 --> 00:24:09,573
Kkot-nim, hold on.

296
00:24:11,199 --> 00:24:14,536
Lee No-ma. If you're going to lie,
get your story straight.

297
00:24:15,328 --> 00:24:16,663
What's wrong with my story?

298
00:24:16,746 --> 00:24:20,250
Ahn Jung-geun assassinated Ito Hirobumi
more than 20 years ago.

299
00:24:20,333 --> 00:24:21,918
If her parents died back then,

300
00:24:22,002 --> 00:24:23,420
how could she be 7 now?

301
00:24:25,422 --> 00:24:26,381
You're right.

302
00:24:26,965 --> 00:24:29,092
If you're going to lie,
make it convincing.

303
00:24:29,176 --> 00:24:30,760
Otherwise, don't even bother.

304
00:24:31,720 --> 00:24:33,805
Even so, how could we possibly tell her…

305
00:24:34,764 --> 00:24:37,100
that her parents caught the disease
from her and died,

306
00:24:37,184 --> 00:24:38,852
and that she's paralyzed for life?

307
00:24:39,603 --> 00:24:42,022
If we tell her that her parents

308
00:24:42,105 --> 00:24:44,941
gave their lives for independence,

309
00:24:45,650 --> 00:24:47,068
she can live with pride like I do.

310
00:24:47,152 --> 00:24:49,946
Pride? What does that even matter?

311
00:24:53,158 --> 00:24:54,034
Kkot-nim.

312
00:24:54,117 --> 00:24:57,204
Don't go telling people
about the independence movement.

313
00:24:57,287 --> 00:25:00,749
No, just forget about it altogether.

314
00:25:00,832 --> 00:25:04,085
You want to eat millet porridge here
or eat rice in an <i>ondol </i>house?

315
00:25:04,169 --> 00:25:05,712
I want rice.

316
00:25:05,795 --> 00:25:06,713
Right?

317
00:25:06,796 --> 00:25:10,008
So you should just
think about living a good life.

318
00:25:10,091 --> 00:25:11,551
Got it?

319
00:25:14,179 --> 00:25:15,305
Hey, Lee Ga-kyung.

320
00:25:15,388 --> 00:25:18,975
What devilish nonsense
are you rattling on about?

321
00:25:19,059 --> 00:25:20,769
The independence movement means nothing?

322
00:25:20,852 --> 00:25:21,853
Are you not one of us?

323
00:25:21,937 --> 00:25:23,313
Why does that matter?

324
00:25:23,396 --> 00:25:27,526
Let's be frank. What did your parents
dying for independence in 1919 get you?

325
00:25:28,985 --> 00:25:30,445
It didn't bring independence.

326
00:25:30,529 --> 00:25:32,614
It just left you orphaned and suffering.

327
00:25:32,697 --> 00:25:33,532
Do you like that?

328
00:25:33,615 --> 00:25:36,868
Still, I'm proud of my parents
for fighting for independence.

329
00:25:36,952 --> 00:25:38,787
I don't envy those
who sold out our country

330
00:25:38,870 --> 00:25:40,830
or the Japs who live comfortably at all.

331
00:25:41,414 --> 00:25:43,208
If I'd been 10 in 1919,

332
00:25:43,291 --> 00:25:45,252
I'd have been out there
shouting for independence.

333
00:25:45,335 --> 00:25:48,171
Yeah, right. Whatever.

334
00:25:48,255 --> 00:25:50,715
Then why aren't you
fighting for independence now?

335
00:25:50,799 --> 00:25:52,842
Why take Japanese passengers
in your rickshaw?

336
00:25:53,552 --> 00:25:54,553
Yeah, why?

337
00:25:56,972 --> 00:25:58,974
- That's different.
- Whatever.

338
00:25:59,057 --> 00:26:00,976
The independence movement
is none of my concern.

339
00:26:01,059 --> 00:26:03,061
I'll save up so I can leave this country.

340
00:26:03,144 --> 00:26:04,229
I'm going to America.

341
00:26:06,773 --> 00:26:07,649
America?

342
00:26:07,732 --> 00:26:09,734
There she goes again with America.

343
00:26:25,292 --> 00:26:26,710
- Watch your step.
- All right.

344
00:26:40,807 --> 00:26:41,641
The bag…

345
00:26:43,059 --> 00:26:43,893
What do we do?

346
00:26:44,811 --> 00:26:48,023
Even if the thief finds what's in there,

347
00:26:48,106 --> 00:26:50,734
we can only hope nothing goes wrong
until after next Thursday.

348
00:27:12,297 --> 00:27:14,215
This is the living room.

349
00:27:20,013 --> 00:27:21,056
And this is…

350
00:27:23,933 --> 00:27:24,976
the dining room.

351
00:27:33,401 --> 00:27:36,154
And the kitchen is further inside.

352
00:27:36,863 --> 00:27:37,947
The bathroom is here.

353
00:27:40,950 --> 00:27:43,328
And this is my room.

354
00:27:48,333 --> 00:27:51,878
Upstairs, there's a living room, a study,
and a larger bedroom.

355
00:28:09,479 --> 00:28:11,064
You can use this room.

356
00:28:12,273 --> 00:28:13,108
All right, then.

357
00:28:16,444 --> 00:28:17,696
Let's share this room.

358
00:28:21,241 --> 00:28:22,075
Pardon?

359
00:28:23,326 --> 00:28:24,494
We're a married couple.

360
00:28:25,245 --> 00:28:28,915
Someone might drop by.
We'd look more natural sharing a room.

361
00:28:29,582 --> 00:28:32,877
I'll keep wearing this belly wrap.

362
00:28:39,426 --> 00:28:41,553
Right. Understood.

363
00:28:57,444 --> 00:28:58,945
Someone stole your wallet?

364
00:29:00,238 --> 00:29:01,781
Yes, I'm sorry.

365
00:29:01,865 --> 00:29:04,242
Was there something important in it?

366
00:29:05,994 --> 00:29:06,870
Not really.

367
00:29:09,414 --> 00:29:12,041
Then don't waste your time
over a little money.

368
00:29:14,586 --> 00:29:15,420
Yes, sir.

369
00:29:36,900 --> 00:29:38,193
Welcome.

370
00:29:38,943 --> 00:29:40,612
Have you been well, Yanagi?

371
00:29:53,291 --> 00:29:54,459
What are you all doing?

372
00:30:20,860 --> 00:30:21,694
Let's go.

373
00:30:26,866 --> 00:30:31,204
<i>If I had a million won</i>

374
00:30:31,746 --> 00:30:35,625
<i>I'd buy a gold hairpin and a fancy ring</i>

375
00:30:35,708 --> 00:30:38,837
<i>And I'd even buy a plane</i>

376
00:30:38,920 --> 00:30:40,630
<i>And fly high above the clouds</i>

377
00:30:40,713 --> 00:30:42,632
<i>Spinning round and round</i>

378
00:30:42,715 --> 00:30:46,803
<i>No, stop dreaming of a million won</i>

379
00:30:46,886 --> 00:30:51,266
<i>Or you'll waste your youth</i>
<i>Chasing a foolish dream</i>

380
00:30:52,642 --> 00:30:53,726
There.

381
00:30:53,810 --> 00:30:55,353
Let's see what's in there.

382
00:30:55,436 --> 00:30:57,480
Come on, something big.

383
00:31:01,776 --> 00:31:02,694
Clothes.

384
00:31:03,820 --> 00:31:04,737
More clothes.

385
00:31:04,821 --> 00:31:07,240
I hid it away
in case it was worth something.

386
00:31:07,323 --> 00:31:08,491
It's just clothes?

387
00:31:11,578 --> 00:31:12,704
My goodness.

388
00:31:13,329 --> 00:31:16,457
My, my. What could this be?

389
00:31:16,541 --> 00:31:18,126
It feels heavy.

390
00:31:19,752 --> 00:31:20,879
Let's see.

391
00:31:22,922 --> 00:31:24,340
What's with the rice?

392
00:31:24,424 --> 00:31:25,592
Wow, rice!

393
00:31:25,675 --> 00:31:27,302
I haven't seen white rice in years.

394
00:31:27,385 --> 00:31:28,553
I'm going to sell this.

395
00:31:28,636 --> 00:31:31,890
Kkot-nim, don't tell me
you want to eat this, okay?

396
00:31:34,058 --> 00:31:35,894
The bag was disappointing,

397
00:31:35,977 --> 00:31:37,186
but I can tell this is it.

398
00:31:37,812 --> 00:31:40,106
I stepped in dog poop earlier.

399
00:31:40,189 --> 00:31:41,816
Dog poop, I'm counting on you.

400
00:31:41,900 --> 00:31:43,818
Yes. Come on, give us a jackpot.

401
00:31:46,946 --> 00:31:47,822
Meh? That's it?

402
00:31:48,489 --> 00:31:49,365
It's three won.

403
00:31:49,449 --> 00:31:52,118
Jeez, only three won
from a first-class passenger?

404
00:31:54,203 --> 00:31:55,580
I need to poop.

405
00:31:55,663 --> 00:31:58,374
Hey, I asked for a big drop,
not a big dump.

406
00:32:00,919 --> 00:32:01,753
Let's go.

407
00:32:03,838 --> 00:32:06,341
Right, you can keep all those clothes.

408
00:32:06,424 --> 00:32:08,676
{\an8}I'll take 10% of that three won.

409
00:32:10,678 --> 00:32:11,971
That makes it…

410
00:32:12,055 --> 00:32:13,348
Yeah, three jeon.

411
00:32:14,515 --> 00:32:16,267
Hey, make sure I get my cut.

412
00:32:16,351 --> 00:32:17,185
Can you hold it?

413
00:32:20,021 --> 00:32:24,525
Hey, one won is 100 jeon<i>,</i>
so ten percent of three won is 30 jeon<i>.</i>

414
00:32:25,902 --> 00:32:28,196
What would he do without me?

415
00:32:29,822 --> 00:32:32,992
{\an8}"Tokyo Imperial University."

416
00:32:34,744 --> 00:32:35,870
"Literature…"

417
00:32:37,789 --> 00:32:39,040
"Department"?

418
00:32:39,123 --> 00:32:39,958
"Department."

419
00:32:40,959 --> 00:32:43,503
"English Literature."

420
00:32:46,673 --> 00:32:49,092
What's a Tokyo Imperial student
doing in Gyeongseong?

421
00:32:50,301 --> 00:32:51,344
His name is…

422
00:32:58,226 --> 00:32:59,477
What?

423
00:32:59,560 --> 00:33:00,728
Isn't this a pawn ticket?

424
00:33:00,812 --> 00:33:02,814
"Jongno Pawnshop."

425
00:33:03,439 --> 00:33:05,608
{\an8}"1919."

426
00:33:05,692 --> 00:33:07,151
"Sin Tae-jin."

427
00:33:09,988 --> 00:33:11,155
Not worth a thing.

428
00:33:15,618 --> 00:33:17,537
What a bust.

429
00:33:30,008 --> 00:33:31,175
What's wrong with this?

430
00:33:57,493 --> 00:33:58,369
A bullet?

431
00:33:59,287 --> 00:34:00,413
It's a bullet.

432
00:34:10,339 --> 00:34:12,300
I knew the dog poop wouldn't let me down.

433
00:34:15,511 --> 00:34:20,475
Three, four, five, six,
seven, eight, nine.

434
00:34:26,731 --> 00:34:30,443
I apologize for not being able to attend
your wife's funeral last year.

435
00:34:30,526 --> 00:34:32,153
Don't worry about it.

436
00:34:32,236 --> 00:34:35,573
No need to come all the way
from Keijo to Tokyo for something trivial.

437
00:34:41,287 --> 00:34:42,789
It's fruitcake.

438
00:34:42,872 --> 00:34:44,916
- Itsukoshi Department Store?
- Yes, sir.

439
00:34:45,583 --> 00:34:48,920
Last time you were in Keijo,
wasn't Itsukoshi a drapery shop?

440
00:34:49,087 --> 00:34:54,759
{\an8}They built and opened
a big Keijo branch on Bonjeong Avenue.

441
00:34:55,885 --> 00:34:58,679
The plaza in front of it became so clean.

442
00:35:00,681 --> 00:35:02,600
The old drapery shop

443
00:35:02,683 --> 00:35:05,770
used to be crawling
with <i>Josenjing </i>pickpockets.

444
00:35:09,357 --> 00:35:10,191
I'm aware.

445
00:35:11,025 --> 00:35:12,860
I used to be one of them myself.

446
00:35:25,248 --> 00:35:26,415
My apologies.

447
00:35:27,333 --> 00:35:28,584
I didn't mean to offend you.

448
00:35:31,337 --> 00:35:32,421
It's quite all right.

449
00:35:33,548 --> 00:35:35,466
I'll take it as a compliment

450
00:35:35,550 --> 00:35:38,803
that Hideo has become
such a fine Japanese citizen.

451
00:35:39,470 --> 00:35:40,304
Indeed.

452
00:35:40,930 --> 00:35:42,515
That's precisely what I meant.

453
00:35:43,558 --> 00:35:45,518
He's not just an ordinary citizen.

454
00:35:45,601 --> 00:35:48,771
He graduated from the Army Academy
and Tokyo Imperial University.

455
00:35:48,855 --> 00:35:52,066
He's an interpreter trainee
for the Government-General of Joseon,

456
00:35:52,150 --> 00:35:54,569
fluent in Japanese, English,
and the Joseon language, no less.

457
00:35:56,737 --> 00:35:59,532
No one would think
that Hideo is from Joseon.

458
00:36:01,242 --> 00:36:04,620
Hideo couldn't speak a word of Japanese
when we first met.

459
00:36:04,704 --> 00:36:05,580
Do you remember?

460
00:36:18,551 --> 00:36:20,386
What do you think you're doing?

461
00:36:21,554 --> 00:36:22,722
What is it?

462
00:36:22,805 --> 00:36:23,973
Pickpocket!

463
00:36:24,932 --> 00:36:26,684
Police!

464
00:36:29,478 --> 00:36:31,189
Why won't you beg me to spare you?

465
00:36:32,398 --> 00:36:33,608
You don't know Japanese.

466
00:36:37,904 --> 00:36:39,697
Why won't you beg me to spare you?

467
00:36:40,698 --> 00:36:42,158
Whether I get caught and killed

468
00:36:43,326 --> 00:36:44,869
or starve to death on the street,

469
00:36:46,078 --> 00:36:47,246
it makes no difference.

470
00:36:50,208 --> 00:36:51,751
Hurry, over there.

471
00:36:53,669 --> 00:36:54,879
What a nuisance.

472
00:36:55,713 --> 00:36:57,006
Is this the pickpocket?

473
00:36:57,715 --> 00:36:59,050
Not this one.

474
00:37:11,312 --> 00:37:12,230
Watch closely.

475
00:37:13,689 --> 00:37:14,649
The pickpocket…

476
00:37:16,901 --> 00:37:17,777
A gun!

477
00:37:22,448 --> 00:37:23,366
…is that kid.

478
00:37:41,634 --> 00:37:43,928
Do you remember that day?

479
00:37:46,555 --> 00:37:47,431
I don't.

480
00:37:48,766 --> 00:37:51,227
It was too long ago. I don't remember.

481
00:38:25,761 --> 00:38:27,596
Can't you sleep?

482
00:38:31,017 --> 00:38:31,892
<i>Shoot.</i>

483
00:38:31,976 --> 00:38:32,810
No!

484
00:38:38,274 --> 00:38:40,526
I've waited 13 years.

485
00:38:43,070 --> 00:38:45,906
Yet waiting a few more days
feels even harder.

486
00:39:04,800 --> 00:39:06,427
<i>- Get out.</i>
<i>- Auntie.</i>

487
00:39:08,512 --> 00:39:09,472
Get out.

488
00:39:09,555 --> 00:39:11,140
You disgust me.

489
00:39:11,223 --> 00:39:12,600
That filthy blood!

490
00:39:12,683 --> 00:39:14,226
Get out of my house!

491
00:40:01,315 --> 00:40:02,483
Weren't you sleeping?

492
00:40:02,566 --> 00:40:03,401
No.

493
00:40:05,152 --> 00:40:06,153
Hey, you know what?

494
00:40:07,279 --> 00:40:08,572
I'm just wondering.

495
00:40:09,990 --> 00:40:13,244
If your money and I both
fell into the water, which would you save?

496
00:40:13,327 --> 00:40:14,161
You.

497
00:40:16,622 --> 00:40:17,498
Really?

498
00:40:18,332 --> 00:40:19,417
Yeah.

499
00:40:19,500 --> 00:40:20,418
Seriously?

500
00:40:23,421 --> 00:40:24,547
Hold on.

501
00:40:28,884 --> 00:40:30,928
Oh, but I was lying!

502
00:40:31,011 --> 00:40:32,763
Why are you always messing with me?

503
00:40:35,433 --> 00:40:38,018
You learned that
from the foreign missionary.

504
00:40:38,102 --> 00:40:39,979
You cross your fingers when you lie.

505
00:40:40,688 --> 00:40:41,981
What if you do this?

506
00:40:42,064 --> 00:40:44,150
It means "wish me luck."

507
00:40:45,025 --> 00:40:46,026
Right.

508
00:40:46,110 --> 00:40:50,364
How does that go from lying
to wishing for good luck?

509
00:40:51,240 --> 00:40:55,494
It means you're wishing for luck
so nobody catches you lying.

510
00:40:57,830 --> 00:40:59,915
I always cross my fingers
every time I pickpocket,

511
00:40:59,999 --> 00:41:01,125
so I won't get caught.

512
00:41:02,126 --> 00:41:03,669
Maybe that's why you never get caught.

513
00:41:05,880 --> 00:41:07,006
By the way,

514
00:41:08,048 --> 00:41:10,676
when you save up enough money,
will you really go to America?

515
00:41:11,969 --> 00:41:13,012
Yeah.

516
00:41:13,095 --> 00:41:15,598
I'm going to leave Joseon, no matter what.

517
00:41:17,892 --> 00:41:19,560
I'm sick and tired of this place.

518
00:41:20,478 --> 00:41:21,687
It's miserable here.

519
00:41:23,731 --> 00:41:24,607
Yeah.

520
00:41:25,191 --> 00:41:27,318
Anywhere should be better than this dump.

521
00:41:28,319 --> 00:41:29,653
But why America?

522
00:41:32,239 --> 00:41:34,200
Just because.

523
00:41:36,410 --> 00:41:37,578
Okay.

524
00:42:02,895 --> 00:42:04,313
- Forty.
- Fifty.

525
00:42:04,396 --> 00:42:05,773
- Forty-five.
- Fifty.

526
00:42:05,856 --> 00:42:07,399
- Forty-five.
- Fifty.

527
00:42:11,362 --> 00:42:12,821
I give up. Fine, 50 jeon<i>.</i>

528
00:42:13,989 --> 00:42:15,241
Thank you.

529
00:42:15,324 --> 00:42:16,951
It's so strange.

530
00:42:17,034 --> 00:42:19,870
Why does it feel like
I always pay you whatever you ask?

531
00:42:21,038 --> 00:42:21,914
Tell me about it.

532
00:42:25,584 --> 00:42:26,502
Mister.

533
00:42:27,795 --> 00:42:30,047
How much do bullets go for?

534
00:42:32,216 --> 00:42:33,551
Bullets?

535
00:42:33,634 --> 00:42:35,761
Those are hard to come by. How many?

536
00:42:39,139 --> 00:42:40,015
Fifty?

537
00:42:42,101 --> 00:42:42,977
Ten won.

538
00:42:43,060 --> 00:42:44,687
Come on, 30.

539
00:42:45,813 --> 00:42:47,273
- Fifteen.
- Thirty.

540
00:42:47,356 --> 00:42:49,191
- Fifteen.
- Thirty.

541
00:42:49,275 --> 00:42:52,570
Fine, 20 won. That's it, 20 won.

542
00:42:52,653 --> 00:42:53,904
I can't go any higher.

543
00:42:55,072 --> 00:42:58,158
Get out of the way. Welcome. Welcome!

544
00:42:59,410 --> 00:43:00,995
Twenty won…

545
00:43:01,078 --> 00:43:03,205
The going rate must be around 40 won then.

546
00:43:06,500 --> 00:43:08,502
Are you saying it's not one of them?

547
00:43:09,295 --> 00:43:10,296
Right.

548
00:43:10,379 --> 00:43:14,633
But I've never heard of a pickpocket
who can speak English.

549
00:43:14,717 --> 00:43:18,470
Most of them can't even read
their own letters.

550
00:43:20,889 --> 00:43:22,600
Then we'll look again.

551
00:43:22,683 --> 00:43:25,936
Once we get her, we'll take her to Jongno
Police Station and notify the residence.

552
00:43:27,021 --> 00:43:28,230
Don't take her to the police.

553
00:43:29,565 --> 00:43:30,482
Then what?

554
00:43:30,566 --> 00:43:32,693
I want you to contact me directly.

555
00:43:32,776 --> 00:43:34,612
The Government-General, not the residence.

556
00:43:36,238 --> 00:43:37,948
Will you deal with her yourself?

557
00:43:38,032 --> 00:43:39,825
Understood, sir.

558
00:43:44,204 --> 00:43:48,959
HANSEONG MEDICINE CLINIC

559
00:43:53,047 --> 00:43:55,841
I'll give a prescription
for a restorative herbal tonic.

560
00:43:56,508 --> 00:43:57,551
Wait here.

561
00:43:57,635 --> 00:43:58,510
Here.

562
00:44:41,261 --> 00:44:43,722
The gun and bullets for that day,

563
00:44:44,682 --> 00:44:46,642
and some rice.

564
00:44:48,143 --> 00:44:50,854
The Japs have taken
every last grain of rice,

565
00:44:50,938 --> 00:44:53,107
so it was very hard to come by.

566
00:44:53,190 --> 00:44:54,233
I hope you enjoy it.

567
00:44:55,067 --> 00:44:58,696
Thank you. I'm sorry
you had to spend such valuable funds.

568
00:44:59,613 --> 00:45:02,866
Forgive me. Those bullets were meant
for you to use later.

569
00:45:02,950 --> 00:45:03,992
No worries.

570
00:45:06,245 --> 00:45:07,454
I just can't stand

571
00:45:07,538 --> 00:45:10,249
the thought of that Sato bastard
eating this precious rice.

572
00:45:10,332 --> 00:45:12,000
I want to soak it in poison.

573
00:45:12,084 --> 00:45:13,085
We can't use poison.

574
00:45:16,296 --> 00:45:19,341
The police will inspect the food
before dinner.

575
00:45:19,425 --> 00:45:22,136
I know. That's why I brought the gun.

576
00:45:22,845 --> 00:45:26,140
Thank you. I'll make sure I succeed.

577
00:45:27,516 --> 00:45:31,311
I deliberately approached and married
an American who knew nothing in Shanghai

578
00:45:31,395 --> 00:45:34,231
and came to Gyeongseong
to assassinate Sato.

579
00:45:34,314 --> 00:45:38,652
We've been apart for three months.
As I've been moody due to the pregnancy,

580
00:45:38,736 --> 00:45:41,113
my husband had no idea
the pregnancy was fake.

581
00:45:41,196 --> 00:45:43,073
You've changed your identity twice.

582
00:45:43,157 --> 00:45:44,742
So they'll never trace the link

583
00:45:44,825 --> 00:45:47,411
between So-ran and the Gugukdan
after the operation.

584
00:45:47,494 --> 00:45:51,498
I'll keep an eye on the situation
from within the Government-General.

585
00:45:58,338 --> 00:45:59,923
During dinner that night…

586
00:46:01,216 --> 00:46:02,342
Here's the cake.

587
00:46:03,469 --> 00:46:04,386
A cake?

588
00:46:04,470 --> 00:46:06,472
My wife makes excellent cakes.

589
00:46:07,514 --> 00:46:09,183
By the way, sir.

590
00:46:17,483 --> 00:46:18,358
Hands off.

591
00:46:32,831 --> 00:46:33,957
What the hell is this?

592
00:46:38,504 --> 00:46:40,547
Do you remember Gyeam-ri?

593
00:46:42,132 --> 00:46:43,008
Gyeam-ri?

594
00:46:43,091 --> 00:46:44,343
{\an8}April 1919!

595
00:46:45,427 --> 00:46:49,973
It's where you shot my daughter
and burned 23 other Joseon people alive.

596
00:46:52,976 --> 00:46:55,103
Pay for it with your life.

597
00:47:14,748 --> 00:47:15,999
Listen, So-ran.

598
00:47:16,083 --> 00:47:18,752
I'll wait in the car nearby that day.

599
00:47:18,836 --> 00:47:22,256
No. Security will be tight,
so we won't be able to get away.

600
00:47:23,006 --> 00:47:23,882
Even so…

601
00:47:24,883 --> 00:47:26,677
Suzuki, won't you have a gun that day?

602
00:47:26,760 --> 00:47:28,262
Don't call me that.

603
00:47:28,345 --> 00:47:30,264
Have you already forgotten my Joseon name?

604
00:47:30,347 --> 00:47:32,057
Suzuki has to stay.

605
00:47:32,140 --> 00:47:33,725
The Gugukdan needs Interpreter Suzuki

606
00:47:33,809 --> 00:47:37,312
who can provide us with information
from within the Government-General.

607
00:47:38,188 --> 00:47:39,523
Besides, Suzuki is the one

608
00:47:39,606 --> 00:47:42,818
who's been anonymously reporting
our activities and achievements

609
00:47:42,901 --> 00:47:43,944
to the newspaper.

610
00:47:44,027 --> 00:47:47,823
Thanks to him, those short articles
tucked into the corners of the newspaper

611
00:47:47,906 --> 00:47:50,826
have been giving the Joseon people
a breath of air and hope.

612
00:47:53,495 --> 00:47:57,207
I know living as a spy for so many years
has been difficult for you.

613
00:47:58,083 --> 00:47:59,835
Please keep doing what you're doing.

614
00:48:01,962 --> 00:48:03,088
Of course.

615
00:48:04,715 --> 00:48:08,218
It took 13 years to track him down

616
00:48:09,595 --> 00:48:11,138
and finally get this chance.

617
00:48:12,764 --> 00:48:16,268
Once I kill him,
there's nothing left for me to live for.

618
00:48:18,520 --> 00:48:20,147
So I have to succeed,

619
00:48:20,731 --> 00:48:22,691
and I'll gladly die alone.

620
00:48:22,774 --> 00:48:26,111
All of you, please welcome
Joseon's independence with joy.

621
00:48:27,821 --> 00:48:29,364
Even if I fail,

622
00:48:30,699 --> 00:48:32,200
I'll be the only one who dies.

623
00:48:44,630 --> 00:48:48,342
{\an8}THREE YEARS AGO, SHANGHAI

624
00:48:59,853 --> 00:49:01,647
Why are you looking for the Gugukdan?

625
00:49:03,231 --> 00:49:04,149
Don't move.

626
00:49:04,733 --> 00:49:05,567
Or I'll shoot.

627
00:49:11,907 --> 00:49:14,534
The Joseon people at the sugarcane farm
in my hometown, Hawaii,

628
00:49:14,618 --> 00:49:17,537
collected 926 dollars
for the independence of our country.

629
00:49:21,750 --> 00:49:23,418
I'm here to deliver this money.

630
00:50:07,838 --> 00:50:09,006
Yes?

631
00:50:22,561 --> 00:50:23,770
"Heewon."

632
00:50:23,854 --> 00:50:26,982
"Hee" means joy, and "won" is garden.
So it's "Garden of Joy."

633
00:50:32,237 --> 00:50:36,033
The former owner, a British reporter,
left this foundation stone in place

634
00:50:36,116 --> 00:50:38,076
when he tore down the <i>hanok</i>
and built this house.

635
00:50:41,580 --> 00:50:43,040
HEEWON

636
00:50:43,123 --> 00:50:44,416
Garden of Joy.

637
00:50:47,210 --> 00:50:48,670
When I saw the name…

638
00:50:54,134 --> 00:50:56,470
I wished I could spend…

639
00:50:58,930 --> 00:50:59,931
many years happily…

640
00:51:05,145 --> 00:51:07,022
here with you.

641
00:51:16,073 --> 00:51:17,741
You will succeed today.

642
00:51:23,163 --> 00:51:24,748
I have a favor to ask.

643
00:51:28,043 --> 00:51:30,128
Please make a <i>hanbok </i>for me.

644
00:51:32,756 --> 00:51:35,133
Lay me to rest in it when I'm gone.

645
00:51:37,219 --> 00:51:39,763
I lost the clothes I'd brought for that.

646
00:51:39,846 --> 00:51:41,431
I can't stop thinking about it.

647
00:51:41,515 --> 00:51:43,100
So please do it for me.

648
00:53:35,503 --> 00:53:37,172
What are you doing?

649
00:53:41,676 --> 00:53:43,637
Please show some basic respect.

650
00:53:46,598 --> 00:53:47,474
Everybody out.

651
00:53:48,934 --> 00:53:49,893
Continue outside.

652
00:53:50,518 --> 00:53:51,353
Yes, sir.

653
00:54:22,217 --> 00:54:24,928
Welcome, Inspector General.

654
00:54:25,011 --> 00:54:25,971
Mr. Suzuki.

655
00:54:26,054 --> 00:54:27,764
- Good to see you.
- Good evening.

656
00:54:33,478 --> 00:54:34,312
Hello.

657
00:54:39,359 --> 00:54:41,069
My son came with me.

658
00:54:41,820 --> 00:54:43,238
I hope you don't mind.

659
00:54:43,321 --> 00:54:45,740
Not at all. He's more than welcome.

660
00:54:54,791 --> 00:54:56,293
Let me pour you a drink.

661
00:54:56,376 --> 00:54:57,294
Thanks.

662
00:55:00,505 --> 00:55:01,506
You too, Mr. Suzuki.

663
00:55:02,173 --> 00:55:03,174
Thank you.

664
00:55:05,552 --> 00:55:06,469
You too, Mr. Sato.

665
00:55:07,637 --> 00:55:09,389
- You don't have to stand--
- No, sir.

666
00:55:09,472 --> 00:55:11,057
And please, call me Hideo.

667
00:55:11,808 --> 00:55:12,851
Would that be all right?

668
00:55:12,934 --> 00:55:13,768
Yes.

669
00:55:21,985 --> 00:55:27,240
I know it's rude to bring a gun here.
I hope you'll kindly forgive me.

670
00:55:27,866 --> 00:55:29,951
Force of habit.
For my father's protection.

671
00:55:36,458 --> 00:55:37,542
I don't mind at all.

672
00:55:40,211 --> 00:55:41,963
A son like him must make you proud.

673
00:55:42,881 --> 00:55:46,092
I hope our baby grows up
to be like you, Hideo.

674
00:55:48,386 --> 00:55:49,679
I certainly hope so.

675
00:55:50,472 --> 00:55:51,431
Well, then.

676
00:55:53,016 --> 00:55:53,850
Cheers.

677
00:55:53,933 --> 00:55:55,435
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

678
00:56:05,779 --> 00:56:07,530
I hope you'll enjoy the food.

679
00:56:08,323 --> 00:56:10,784
It looks absolutely sumptuous.

680
00:56:10,867 --> 00:56:13,912
Thank you for preparing
such a nice meal tonight.

681
00:56:14,662 --> 00:56:16,539
My wife doesn't speak Japanese well.

682
00:56:16,623 --> 00:56:17,999
Mr. Inspector General said--

683
00:56:18,792 --> 00:56:19,626
That's right.

684
00:56:20,460 --> 00:56:22,337
I completely forgot.

685
00:56:23,171 --> 00:56:24,798
Let's switch to the Joseon language then.

686
00:56:26,424 --> 00:56:29,219
Everything looks absolutely incredible.

687
00:56:32,263 --> 00:56:33,181
Thank you.

688
00:56:34,682 --> 00:56:36,768
Let me serve you some soup.

689
00:56:36,851 --> 00:56:38,895
I should have skipped lunch today.

690
00:56:38,978 --> 00:56:40,522
I skipped it.

691
00:56:41,106 --> 00:56:42,023
You did? Good.

692
00:56:44,442 --> 00:56:47,028
How many dishes did you make today?

693
00:56:54,411 --> 00:56:55,245
Six.

694
00:56:56,246 --> 00:56:57,080
Six dishes.

695
00:56:58,915 --> 00:57:00,417
I made six dishes.

696
00:57:02,293 --> 00:57:04,421
She even baked a cake for dessert.

697
00:57:04,504 --> 00:57:05,380
Even a cake?

698
00:57:07,924 --> 00:57:10,218
For some reason,
your wife seems like a Joseon person.

699
00:57:18,226 --> 00:57:21,354
But the way she counted on her fingers
tells me she really is Chinese.

700
00:57:27,235 --> 00:57:30,488
I learned how to count with my fingers
the Chinese way

701
00:57:30,572 --> 00:57:31,948
while I was in Shanghai.

702
00:57:33,491 --> 00:57:34,492
Six.

703
00:57:35,160 --> 00:57:36,828
This is how the Chinese do it.

704
00:57:36,911 --> 00:57:38,663
Joseon does it this way.

705
00:57:38,746 --> 00:57:40,582
And Japan does it this way.

706
00:57:40,665 --> 00:57:41,958
That's right.

707
00:57:43,042 --> 00:57:44,461
Did you know the difference?

708
00:57:45,128 --> 00:57:47,464
Yes, I learned it at the Army Academy.

709
00:57:54,304 --> 00:57:56,973
I ordered a cake
from Itsukoshi Department Store.

710
00:57:57,640 --> 00:58:00,518
I didn't know your wife had baked a cake,

711
00:58:00,602 --> 00:58:02,020
so I made a mistake.

712
00:58:02,103 --> 00:58:03,688
Not at all. It's thoughtful of you.

713
00:58:03,771 --> 00:58:05,106
Thank you.

714
00:58:05,815 --> 00:58:06,983
I'll…

715
00:58:07,066 --> 00:58:09,611
I'll get the door.
Please continue, gentlemen.

716
00:58:09,694 --> 00:58:10,653
Would you?

717
00:58:16,659 --> 00:58:17,869
- All right.
- Yes.

718
00:58:25,126 --> 00:58:26,169
Where's the cake…?

719
00:58:26,252 --> 00:58:29,297
She says she's a live-in maid here.

720
00:58:29,380 --> 00:58:30,465
Is that true?

721
00:58:31,758 --> 00:58:33,927
He doesn't speak this language.

722
00:58:34,010 --> 00:58:36,429
I'll give the bullets back.
Just give me 60 won.

723
00:58:37,847 --> 00:58:39,307
So it's you. The pickpocket.

724
00:58:39,390 --> 00:58:41,893
If you stall now, he'll catch on.

725
00:58:41,976 --> 00:58:43,061
You'll pay me, right?

726
00:58:45,688 --> 00:58:46,856
Ma'am?

727
00:58:47,440 --> 00:58:49,776
Is she your live-in maid?

728
00:58:52,737 --> 00:58:53,738
Come in.

729
00:58:55,156 --> 00:58:56,783
I'm going in, then.

730
00:59:03,706 --> 00:59:06,626
The taxi driver at Gyeongseong Station
remembered you right away.

731
00:59:07,418 --> 00:59:11,756
He said a lady who looked great in a <i>qipao</i>
got off at a very nice house.

732
00:59:13,174 --> 00:59:15,343
This house is so amazing.

733
00:59:16,302 --> 00:59:17,262
Whatever you're up to--

734
00:59:17,345 --> 00:59:20,765
If you lock me up or threaten me,
I'll scream and make a scene.

735
00:59:21,599 --> 00:59:23,851
Then they'll all hear it.

736
00:59:24,477 --> 00:59:25,311
Right?

737
00:59:26,938 --> 00:59:29,357
The Government-General's number two
is here.

738
00:59:33,528 --> 00:59:35,238
Give me 60 won,

739
00:59:35,321 --> 00:59:36,573
and I'll leave quietly.

740
00:59:46,541 --> 00:59:48,459
Sixty won, not six.

741
00:59:49,043 --> 00:59:50,211
That's all I have now.

742
00:59:50,295 --> 00:59:52,171
Wait here quietly,
and I'll get the rest later.

743
00:59:54,591 --> 00:59:55,592
All right.

744
00:59:57,093 --> 00:59:59,053
I'll bring the bullets tomorrow.

745
00:59:59,137 --> 01:00:01,723
I didn't bring them
since there'd be police everywhere.

746
01:00:02,432 --> 01:00:04,058
Stay here until I get back.

747
01:00:04,142 --> 01:00:05,518
Don't try anything stupid.

748
01:00:06,894 --> 01:00:07,895
Stupid?

749
01:00:09,856 --> 01:00:11,107
Don't worry.

750
01:00:11,190 --> 01:00:14,277
I couldn't care less
what you'll do with those bullets.

751
01:00:16,696 --> 01:00:19,115
It's not like
anyone's going to pay me to tell

752
01:00:19,198 --> 01:00:22,118
the Government-General's number two

753
01:00:22,201 --> 01:00:24,078
that you smuggled bullets in.

754
01:00:25,538 --> 01:00:26,581
I'll be waiting.

755
01:00:28,958 --> 01:00:30,710
You have quite an eye.

756
01:00:31,210 --> 01:00:32,712
Oh, you think so?

757
01:00:44,641 --> 01:00:46,893
You took so long,
I was about to send him after you.

758
01:00:46,976 --> 01:00:48,603
Is there a problem with the cake?

759
01:00:49,437 --> 01:00:52,523
No, it wasn't the cake.
It was our live-in maid.

760
01:00:55,610 --> 01:00:57,362
I sent her on an errand earlier.

761
01:00:57,445 --> 01:00:59,697
I scolded her for staying out late
playing around.

762
01:01:01,157 --> 01:01:02,283
Again?

763
01:01:03,785 --> 01:01:05,078
The food is delicious.

764
01:01:05,870 --> 01:01:06,704
Thank you.

765
01:01:15,797 --> 01:01:19,008
I said 60 so I could come down to 50,
but she didn't even haggle.

766
01:01:20,593 --> 01:01:21,969
I should've started at 100.

767
01:01:59,757 --> 01:02:01,217
An American passport?

768
01:02:11,853 --> 01:02:12,937
It's real.

769
01:02:26,492 --> 01:02:27,702
By the way, sir,

770
01:02:28,286 --> 01:02:30,455
I always think about
how I can be of service

771
01:02:30,538 --> 01:02:32,248
to the Government-General.

772
01:02:32,331 --> 01:02:33,875
So I wanted to discuss--

773
01:02:33,958 --> 01:02:35,460
- What is it?
- Yes.

774
01:02:39,338 --> 01:02:41,841
I suppose
this is a rather sensitive issue.

775
01:02:43,718 --> 01:02:47,930
So I was wondering if the others
could give us a moment alone.

776
01:02:49,265 --> 01:02:51,809
Is it something these two shouldn't hear?

777
01:02:53,144 --> 01:02:54,479
If you don't mind.

778
01:02:55,813 --> 01:02:56,814
Very well.

779
01:02:57,732 --> 01:02:59,233
Give us the room. You too.

780
01:02:59,317 --> 01:03:00,526
Sure.

781
01:03:09,660 --> 01:03:11,162
Can you step out for a moment?

782
01:03:12,079 --> 01:03:14,081
All right. Then…

783
01:03:15,374 --> 01:03:16,667
I'll go get the cake.

784
01:03:41,442 --> 01:03:43,486
Excuse me, where's the bathroom?

785
01:03:43,569 --> 01:03:44,946
Right this way.

786
01:03:46,614 --> 01:03:48,574
I'll go check on the kitchen.

787
01:03:48,658 --> 01:03:49,492
All right.

788
01:04:01,754 --> 01:04:04,048
Keep Hideo distracted in the living room.

789
01:04:25,778 --> 01:04:26,612
I'm sorry.

790
01:04:44,130 --> 01:04:45,131
Hello.

791
01:04:46,757 --> 01:04:48,384
I didn't expect to see you here.

792
01:04:52,597 --> 01:04:56,100
Gyeongseong really is a small place.

793
01:04:57,560 --> 01:04:58,561
Excuse me?

794
01:05:00,605 --> 01:05:03,065
I was impressed
by your pickpocketing skills.

795
01:05:06,277 --> 01:05:07,653
I'm not sure what you mean.

796
01:05:08,863 --> 01:05:11,115
It's a compliment.
I was genuinely impressed.

797
01:05:11,699 --> 01:05:13,117
I was quite hopeless myself.

798
01:05:14,368 --> 01:05:16,287
Would you mind telling me your secret?

799
01:05:16,370 --> 01:05:18,247
It might come in handy someday.

800
01:05:20,082 --> 01:05:21,834
I'm afraid I don't understand.

801
01:05:22,960 --> 01:05:26,589
I work here as a live-in maid.

802
01:05:27,632 --> 01:05:28,466
I see.

803
01:05:29,508 --> 01:05:32,720
Come to think of it,
she said she was from mainland Japan.

804
01:05:34,430 --> 01:05:37,099
I must have mistaken you for someone else.

805
01:05:38,059 --> 01:05:39,602
All right, then.

806
01:05:41,562 --> 01:05:43,439
What did you steal upstairs just now?

807
01:05:47,026 --> 01:05:50,154
Why do you keep accusing me of…

808
01:05:50,988 --> 01:05:51,822
stealing?

809
01:05:52,698 --> 01:05:54,033
If you keep suspecting me,

810
01:05:54,116 --> 01:05:57,203
I'll take off my clothes
and prove my innocence.

811
01:05:58,037 --> 01:05:58,996
No need.

812
01:05:59,789 --> 01:06:00,957
I apologize.

813
01:06:05,836 --> 01:06:07,129
That reminds me.

814
01:06:07,213 --> 01:06:09,173
Did you see the princess's photo?

815
01:06:12,093 --> 01:06:13,010
My wallet.

816
01:06:14,512 --> 01:06:15,388
I'd like it back.

817
01:06:16,764 --> 01:06:18,474
I don't know what you're talking--

818
01:06:18,557 --> 01:06:19,892
Enough with the act.

819
01:06:27,525 --> 01:06:30,027
Just return my wallet,
and we can end this quietly.

820
01:06:31,529 --> 01:06:33,447
Making a scene
won't do either of us any good.

821
01:06:37,368 --> 01:06:38,369
Who is it?

822
01:06:40,579 --> 01:06:41,747
And you are…

823
01:06:43,624 --> 01:06:45,084
Are you the live-in maid?

824
01:06:46,002 --> 01:06:48,045
Yes, I'm the maid.

825
01:06:49,797 --> 01:06:51,632
I was on my way out on an errand.

826
01:06:58,389 --> 01:06:59,265
You startled me.

827
01:07:01,225 --> 01:07:02,184
How can I help you?

828
01:07:03,102 --> 01:07:06,814
Are you expecting a cake
from Itsukoshi Department Store?

829
01:07:24,749 --> 01:07:27,293
So, what exactly is this sensitive issue?

830
01:07:28,502 --> 01:07:29,420
Right.

831
01:07:32,298 --> 01:07:34,133
Yesterday, President Hoover--

832
01:07:34,216 --> 01:07:35,176
One moment.

833
01:07:35,259 --> 01:07:37,094
Let me use the bathroom first.

834
01:07:37,178 --> 01:07:38,596
I've had too much to drink.

835
01:07:41,849 --> 01:07:42,933
That's right.

836
01:07:44,435 --> 01:07:46,312
The inspector general ordered it.

837
01:07:46,395 --> 01:07:47,605
Yes, sir.

838
01:07:49,607 --> 01:07:51,984
Aren't you going to get the cake?

839
01:07:52,860 --> 01:07:53,694
Sure.

840
01:07:54,653 --> 01:07:55,488
Give it to me.

841
01:07:57,990 --> 01:07:59,116
The cake is here.

842
01:08:29,188 --> 01:08:30,481
The cake is…

843
01:08:45,579 --> 01:08:46,872
Where's the bathroom?

844
01:08:46,956 --> 01:08:48,249
It's that way.

845
01:09:05,474 --> 01:09:07,226
- Hideo.
- Yes.

846
01:09:15,568 --> 01:09:18,112
It's the second door down the hall.

847
01:09:28,539 --> 01:09:31,167
Bring the cake when I call for you.

848
01:09:34,295 --> 01:09:36,922
I wonder if Mr. Yoo has a cigarette.

849
01:09:43,345 --> 01:09:44,430
Come in.

850
01:10:04,742 --> 01:10:06,493
She's the pickpocket
who stole the bullets?

851
01:10:07,494 --> 01:10:08,329
Yes.

852
01:10:08,412 --> 01:10:11,916
But once I kill Sato,
it won't matter what she says.

853
01:10:12,625 --> 01:10:14,293
I'll shoot Sato when he walks in.

854
01:10:14,376 --> 01:10:16,337
Philip will be my hostage.

855
01:10:25,512 --> 01:10:28,933
Excuse me, I need to head out soon.

856
01:10:29,016 --> 01:10:31,644
You can put the cake over there…

857
01:10:33,145 --> 01:10:34,438
Take me to the bathroom.

858
01:10:36,357 --> 01:10:38,275
Make a sound, and you'll die first.

859
01:10:45,491 --> 01:10:47,534
I'll leave the rest to you.

860
01:10:47,618 --> 01:10:48,702
Thank you for everything.

861
01:11:39,503 --> 01:11:42,089
Wait. We're not done talking.

862
01:11:50,139 --> 01:11:51,140
What?

863
01:13:02,961 --> 01:13:05,589
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

864
01:13:07,883 --> 01:13:08,717
{\an8}<i>Who are you really?</i>

865
01:13:12,137 --> 01:13:14,640
{\an8}<i>Where did he run off to? Say it!</i>

866
01:13:14,723 --> 01:13:16,266
{\an8}<i>Think carefully.</i>

867
01:13:16,350 --> 01:13:18,936
{\an8}Give me what's in my wallet,
and I'll let this all go.

868
01:13:19,019 --> 01:13:21,105
{\an8}<i>I don't trust </i>Josenjing<i>.</i>

869
01:13:21,188 --> 01:13:23,357
{\an8}If you get caught,
do you think I'll be safe?

870
01:13:24,108 --> 01:13:25,567
{\an8}<i>They're looking for her.</i>

871
01:13:25,651 --> 01:13:28,112
{\an8}<i>For 13 years,</i>
<i>I didn't know if he was alive.</i>

872
01:13:28,195 --> 01:13:29,238
{\an8}Keum Seo-hee!

873
01:13:29,321 --> 01:13:31,615
{\an8}Could this be some kind of fate?

874
01:13:32,574 --> 01:13:35,244
{\an8}<i>There's no way we'll see each other again.</i>

875
01:13:36,120 --> 01:13:37,371
{\an8}<i>Do you want to get rid of her?</i>

876
01:13:44,837 --> 01:13:46,839
{\an8}Subtitle translation by: Youji Kim

